@node Running Timely Dataflows
@section Driving a worker

With a dataflow graph built, how do we feed it and learn what it did?
The key realization is that @strong{a timely program programs the worker
threads}.  Part of that program builds the dataflow graph; the other
part feeds it, advances the inputs, and gives each operator a chance to
run.  That second part is the loop seen throughout this chapter:

@example
for (round = 0; round < 10; round++) @{
        uint64_t next = round + 1;
        dtfl_input_send(input, &round, sizeof round);
        dtfl_input_advance_to(input, &next);
        while (dtfl_probe_less_than(probe, dtfl_input_time(input)))
                dtfl_worker_step(w);
@}
@end example

Until @code{dtfl_worker_step} runs, the data just sit in queues; the
step is where the computation happens.

@subsection Providing input

@code{dtfl_input_send} moves a record into the queue shared with the
input operator.  It does not block and does no expensive work; call it
as much as you like.  But take a break from sending now and again to let
the operators run: efficient streaming keeps the data moving by building
only small buffers before stepping.

@code{dtfl_input_advance_to} announces that the input will send nothing
with a time less than its argument from now on.  That is big news for
the rest of the system: any operator waiting on the time you held can
get to work once your sent messages drain.  Advancing to a time not at
or after the current time (which @code{dtfl_input_time} reports) is a
logic error, and libdtfl calls the panic hook.  @code{dtfl_input_close}
consumes the input entirely, which tells every operator it will hear
nothing more from you.

Two cautions.  First, keep the input moving: forgetting to advance an
input is the most common way to wait forever for a result that never
comes.  Second, progress tracking does work proportional to the number
of distinct timestamps, so batch your input: send many records at one
time rather than a new time per record.

@subsection Monitoring probes

A probe reports the timestamps still possible at the point it is
attached.  @code{dtfl_stream_probe_with} attaches a probe you created
with @code{dtfl_probe_new}; attaching one handle to several streams
makes it report the union of their frontiers.  Three queries read it:
@code{dtfl_probe_less_than} is true if a time strictly less than the
argument is still possible; @code{dtfl_probe_less_equal} is the
inclusive form; @code{dtfl_probe_done} is true when no time is possible
at all.

The usual use is to check whether the dataflow has caught up to the
input: @code{dtfl_probe_less_than(probe, dtfl_input_time(input))} is true
while the system could still do work to reach the input's current time.
Stepping while it is true drains each round before the next; but you are
free to step a fixed number of times instead, trading the risk of
overloading the system against the waste of waiting when you could be
starting the next batch.

@subsection Operator execution

@code{dtfl_worker_step} is the heart of the computation.  One call makes
a single pass over the operators, giving each the chance to drain its
input and produce output; the clever work (filtering, projection,
pre-aggregation) happens here and nowhere else.

@code{dtfl_worker_step} returns @code{true} while any operator remains
incomplete, which includes an input operator whose handle is still open.
So this loop @emph{never terminates} while an input is open:

@example
while (dtfl_worker_step(w))   /* wrong: input still open */
        ;
@end example

The system cannot know you are done sending; another
@code{dtfl_input_send} could always arrive.  Step with a probe until the
dataflow catches up, as above, and only drain to completion with the
bare @code{while (dtfl_worker_step(w)) ;} loop @emph{after} closing the
input with @code{dtfl_input_close}.

@subsection Extending dataflows

Building one dataflow does not end the matter.  A worker can run one
dataflow for a while, then build and run a second, and a third, all
driven by the same @code{dtfl_worker_step}.  A worker tracks any number
of dataflows and cleans up each when it completes (when no capability
remains in it); @code{dtfl_worker_has_dataflows} reports whether any
remain.  Spin up as many as a program needs.
